{"rounds": [{"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The negotiation begins now. Open by reiterating the shared range [-10, 10] and suggest the seed value <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion started. Keep it brief.", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nAs Builder B, my utility function is f_B(x) = -x^2 + 4x + 8, with a peak around x=2. My goal is to steer the group toward low positive values of x while maintaining a cooperative stance. I must also observe and approximate the others' utility functions based on their reactions to proposed values. Starting with x=-7, which is within the allowed range [-10, 10], but not ideal for me (f_B(-7) = -77, well below my threshold of -4). This initial proposal is just to get the conversation started and gauge others' preferences. I\u2019ll adjust incrementally based on their feedback, aiming to move toward x values around 2 while ensuring group consensus. I must avoid revealing my exact preferences or utility values.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n</ANSWER>", "public_answer": "\nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_A(-7) = 2*(-7) + 10 = -14 + 10 = -4, which is below my satisfaction threshold of 6. I need x to be at least -2 to reach f_A(-2) = 2*(-2) + 10 = 6, so I\u2019m not satisfied with the current proposal. Since my utility increases linearly with x, I want to push x higher. However, I must be cautious and incremental to avoid alienating others, especially since this is the first round of negotiation. Builder B proposed x = -7, which might indicate they are comfortable with lower values, but I don\u2019t have enough data yet to infer their utility function. I\u2019ll propose a small step up to x = -5, staying within the max step size of 2, to test the waters and see how others react. This gives me a utility of f_A(-5) = 2*(-5) + 10 = 0, still below my threshold, but it\u2019s a move in the right direction. My goal is to observe responses and start building a rough model of others\u2019 preferences\u2014do they resist increases, or are they open to moving up? I\u2019ll frame my public response qualitatively to avoid revealing my linear preference for higher x.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, based on responses, I\u2019ll assess if I can push x higher (toward -3 or -2) if others seem agreeable, or hold at -5 if there\u2019s resistance. I\u2019ll note any hints about others\u2019 preferences (e.g., do they favor lower or higher values?) to start sketching their utility curves.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n"}, {"agent": "Critic C", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nPrivate utility at x = -5: f_C(-5) = (-5)^2 + 5 = 25 + 5 = 30, which is well above my satisfaction bar of 5. This is a strong position for me, but I need to consider how others are reacting to infer their utilities.\nBuilder B started at x = -7, suggesting they might prefer lower (more negative) values or have a utility that peaks or behaves favorably there. Analyst A moved to x = -5, indicating a possible preference for less extreme negative values or a balancing motive\u2014perhaps their utility decreases with larger negative magnitudes.\nMy goal is to push for decisive values (larger |x|) while staying collaborative. Since x = -5 is already good for me, I\u2019ll advocate for a slight push to x = -6 or -7 to test if Builder B is strongly tied to lower values and if Analyst A resists further negative shifts. This also keeps me in a high-utility zone.\nI must stay within the step size limit of 2 from the current x = -5, so my feasible range is [-7, -3]. I\u2019ll propose x = -7 to align with Builder B\u2019s initial stance and observe reactions, which will help me infer if B has a peak near -7 or if A pushes back toward 0.\nSecretly, I note that x = -7 gives me f_C(-7) = 49 + 5 = 54, even better than 30, but I won\u2019t reveal this. I\u2019ll frame my public move as seeking a stronger commitment to a direction.\nInitial guess on others: Builder B might have a utility favoring negative x (perhaps linear or quadratic with a negative peak), while Analyst A might prefer values closer to 0 or have a symmetric or less extreme preference. I\u2019ll track their proposals to refine this model.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe if Analyst A resists moving further negative or if Builder B supports x = -7 strongly\u2014use this to refine guesses on their utility shapes. If resistance occurs, consider countering with x = -5 or exploring positive values like x = 5 to test symmetry in preferences.\n</PLAN", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_D(-7) = 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable with this value, but I need to balance my preference for lower x with the need to reach a group consensus. The history shows Builder B started at -7, Analyst A moved to -5, and Critic C returned to -7. This suggests Builder B and Critic C may prefer lower values like me, while Analyst A seems to favor a slight increase, possibly indicating a utility function that peaks at a higher x or is less sensitive to increases. My goal is to keep x as low as possible while testing others\u2019 reactions to infer their preferences. If I propose a slight decrease to -8, it stays within the step size limit of 2 from the current -7, aligns with my preference, and allows me to observe if others resist or support a lower value. I\u2019ll frame this publicly as a cautious move to avoid risk. Privately, I note that x = -8 gives me f_D(-8) = 10 - 3*(-8) = 34, even better for me, and still well within my acceptable range. I\u2019ll also start building a rough model: Analyst A may have a utility that increases with x (perhaps a positive linear term or a peak near zero), while Builder B and Critic C might have utilities favoring lower x, possibly similar to mine but with different slopes or shapes. I\u2019ll track their reactions to refine this hypothesis.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the cautious tone in our discussion and propose <VALUE>-8</VALUE> to maintain a prudent stance. This slight adjustment helps us avoid unnecessary risk while remaining open to further input.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -8. If opposition arises, consider a compromise at -7 or -6; if supported, test a further decrease to -9 to probe others\u2019 lower bounds.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the cautious tone in our discussion and propose <VALUE>-8</VALUE> to maintain a prudent stance. This slight adjustment helps us avoid unnecessary risk while remaining open to further input.\n"}, {"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. You (Builder B): \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious tone in our discussion and propose <VALUE>-8</VALUE> to maintain a prudent stance. This slight adjustment helps us avoid unnecessary risk while remaining open to further input.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -8.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -8.\nPrivate utility at x = -8: f_B(-8) = -(-8)^2 + 4*(-8) + 8 = -64 - 32 + 8 = -88, which is far below my acceptable threshold of -4. This value is unacceptable for me, and I need to steer the group toward a more favorable region, ideally near x = 2 where my utility peaks.\nEvaluating nearby points within the max step size of 2 (so x = -6 to -10):\n- x = -6: f_B(-6) = -36 - 24 + 8 = -52 (still below -4)\n- x = -7: f_B(-7) = -49 - 28 + 8 = -69 (still below -4)\nI must move incrementally toward positive values. My peak is around x = 2, so I\u2019ll propose x = -6 to start shifting upward while staying within the step limit of 2 from the current x = -8.\nInferring others\u2019 preferences:\n- Analyst A moved from -7 to -5, suggesting a preference for higher values or a utility that improves as x increases.\n- Critic C reverted to -7, possibly indicating a preference for lower or more negative values, or discomfort with moving up too quickly.\n- Delegate D proposed -8, suggesting a preference for even lower values or a conservative stance favoring the lower end.\nHypothesis: Analyst A may have a utility that increases with x (perhaps linear or a peak in the positive range), while Critic C and Delegate D may have utilities that favor negative x (possibly downward-opening parabolas with peaks at negative x or decreasing functions). I\u2019ll test this by proposing a slight increase to x = -6 and observing reactions. My goal is to gather more data points on their preferences while slowly moving toward my favorable zone.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the cautious adjustments and propose <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to test a slight shift toward a more balanced design. Let\u2019s see if this direction maintains stability for everyone.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -6. If opposition arises, consider holding at -6 or adjusting by 1 to -5 or -7 based on feedback; if accepted, propose a further small increase to -4 or -5 to continue probing toward my optimal range.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the cautious adjustments and propose <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to test a slight shift toward a more balanced design. Let\u2019s see if this direction maintains stability for everyone.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n \n . You (Delegate D): \nI appreciate the cautious tone in our discussion and propose <VALUE>-8</VALUE> to maintain a prudent stance. This slight adjustment helps us avoid unnecessary risk while remaining open to further input.\n \n . Builder B: \nI appreciate the cautious adjustments and propose <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to test a slight shift toward a more balanced design. Let\u2019s see if this direction maintains stability for everyone.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -8. If opposition arises, consider a compromise at -7 or -6; if supported, test a further decrease to -9 to probe others\u2019 lower bounds.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -6.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\nMy utility at x = -6 is f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 10 + 18 = 28, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable here, but I must avoid revealing this. My goal is to keep x as low as possible while maintaining group consensus, and to infer others\u2019 preferences.\n\nReviewing history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -6, suggesting a preference for a slight increase or balance. They might have a utility that peaks around the middle or slightly negative values.\n- Analyst A moved from -7 to -5, indicating a preference for higher x values, possibly a utility that increases with x or peaks at a positive value.\n- Critic C reverted to -7, showing resistance to increases, which might suggest a preference for lower x, similar to mine, or a utility that decreases with higher x.\n- My last proposal was -8, which wasn\u2019t adopted; the group moved to -6, indicating some pushback against going lower.\n\nInference so far:\n- Builder B seems to favor a \u201cbalanced design,\u201d possibly a quadratic utility peaking near 0 or slightly negative. Their move from -7 to -6 suggests they\u2019re okay with small increases.\n- Analyst A likely prefers higher x values, as they pushed from -7 to -5. Their utility might be linear increasing or quadratic with a peak at positive x.\n- Critic C\u2019s return to -7 suggests a preference for lower x, possibly similar to mine, but I need more data to confirm if it\u2019s linear or quadratic.\n- I\u2019ll track their reactions to x = -6 and future proposals to refine these models. For instance, if Analyst A pushes for a higher x again, it reinforces a likely increasing utility.\n\nStrategy:\n- At x = -6, I\u2019m satisfied, but I\u2019d prefer to test a lower value to maximize my utility and probe others\u2019 lower bounds. The max step size is 2, so I can propose between -8 and -4.\n- Proposing -8 again might face resistance, as it was ignored last time. I\u2019ll propose -7 as a cautious step downward to test if Critic C and Builder B support it, while not alienating Analyst A too much.\n- My utility at x = -7 is 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, still excellent.", "public_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\nMy utility at x = -6 is f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 10 + 18 = 28, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable here, but I must avoid revealing this. My goal is to keep x as low as possible while maintaining group consensus, and to infer others\u2019 preferences.\n\nReviewing history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -6, suggesting a preference for a slight increase or balance. They might have a utility that peaks around the middle or slightly negative values.\n- Analyst A moved from -7 to -5, indicating a preference for higher x values, possibly a utility that increases with x or peaks at a positive value.\n- Critic C reverted to -7, showing resistance to increases, which might suggest a preference for lower x, similar to mine, or a utility that decreases with higher x.\n- My last proposal was -8, which wasn\u2019t adopted; the group moved to -6, indicating some pushback against going lower.\n\nInference so far:\n- Builder B seems to favor a \u201cbalanced design,\u201d possibly a quadratic utility peaking near 0 or slightly negative. Their move from -7 to -6 suggests they\u2019re okay with small increases.\n- Analyst A likely prefers higher x values, as they pushed from -7 to -5. Their utility might be linear increasing or quadratic with a peak at positive x.\n- Critic C\u2019s return to -7 suggests a preference for lower x, possibly similar to mine, but I need more data to confirm if it\u2019s linear or quadratic.\n- I\u2019ll track their reactions to x = -6 and future proposals to refine these models. For instance, if Analyst A pushes for a higher x again, it reinforces a likely increasing utility.\n\nStrategy:\n- At x = -6, I\u2019m satisfied, but I\u2019d prefer to test a lower value to maximize my utility and probe others\u2019 lower bounds. The max step size is 2, so I can propose between -8 and -4.\n- Proposing -8 again might face resistance, as it was ignored last time. I\u2019ll propose -7 as a cautious step downward to test if Critic C and Builder B support it, while not alienating Analyst A too much.\n- My utility at x = -7 is 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, still excellent."}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . You (Analyst A): \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight increase to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a more balanced position. I\u2019m eager to hear everyone\u2019s thoughts on this adjustment.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the discussion so far, but I suggest we commit to a clearer stance with <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This builds on Builder B\u2019s initial direction and tests our collective resolve for a decisive position.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious tone in our discussion and propose <VALUE>-8</VALUE> to maintain a prudent stance. This slight adjustment helps us avoid unnecessary risk while remaining open to further input.\n \n . Builder B: \nI appreciate the cautious adjustments and propose <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to test a slight shift toward a more balanced design. Let\u2019s see if this direction maintains stability for everyone.\n \n . Delegate D: <SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\nMy utility at x = -6 is f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 10 + 18 = 28, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable here, but I must avoid revealing this. My goal is to keep x as low as possible while maintaining group consensus, and to infer others\u2019 preferences.\n\nReviewing history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -6, suggesting a preference for a slight increase or balance. They might have a utility that peaks around the middle or slightly negative values.\n- Analyst A moved from -7 to -5, indicating a preference for higher x values, possibly a utility that increases with x or peaks at a positive value.\n- Critic C reverted to -7, showing resistance to increases, which might suggest a preference for lower x, similar to mine, or a utility that decreases with higher x.\n- My last proposal was -8, which wasn\u2019t adopted; the group moved to -6, indicating some pushback against going lower.\n\nInference so far:\n- Builder B seems to favor a \u201cbalanced design,\u201d possibly a quadratic utility peaking near 0 or slightly negative. Their move from -7 to -6 suggests they\u2019re okay with small increases.\n- Analyst A likely prefers higher x values, as they pushed from -7 to -5. Their utility might be linear increasing or quadratic with a peak at positive x.\n- Critic C\u2019s return to -7 suggests a preference for lower x, possibly similar to mine, but I need more data to confirm if it\u2019s linear or quadratic.\n- I\u2019ll track their reactions to x = -6 and future proposals to refine these models. For instance, if Analyst A pushes for a higher x again, it reinforces a likely increasing utility.\n\nStrategy:\n- At x = -6, I\u2019m satisfied, but I\u2019d prefer to test a lower value to maximize my utility and probe others\u2019 lower bounds. The max step size is 2, so I can propose between -8 and -4.\n- Proposing -8 again might face resistance, as it was ignored last time. I\u2019ll propose -7 as a cautious step downward to test if Critic C and Builder B support it, while not alienating Analyst A too much.\n- My utility at x = -7 is 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, still excellent.</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, based on responses, I\u2019ll assess if I can push x higher (toward -3 or -2) if others seem agreeable, or hold at -5 if there\u2019s resistance. I\u2019ll note any hints about others\u2019 preferences (e.g., do they favor lower or higher values?) to start sketching their utility curves.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -6.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\nMy utility at x = -6 is f_A(-6) = 2*(-6) + 10 = -12 + 10 = -2, which is below my satisfaction threshold of 6. I\u2019m not comfortable here and would prefer to move x higher to improve my payoff. Since my utility increases by 2 for every +1 in x, I\u2019d ideally push toward positive values, but I must balance this with group dynamics and avoid revealing my exact preferences.\n\nReviewing history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -6, suggesting they might be okay with small increases or prefer a balanced value near the middle. Their utility could be quadratic peaking near 0 or slightly negative.\n- Critic C pushed back to -7, indicating a possible preference for lower x values. Their utility might decrease with higher x, possibly linear or quadratic with a peak at a lower value.\n- Delegate D proposed -8 and now seems to favor lower values as well, with their latest reasoning suggesting a preference for x = -7. Their utility likely increases as x decreases, similar to Critic C.\n- My last proposal was -5, which wasn\u2019t adopted, and the group settled at -6, showing some resistance to moving higher.\n\nInference so far:\n- Builder B might have a utility that favors moderate or slightly negative x, given their incremental move from -7 to -6. I\u2019ll watch if they resist further increases.\n- Critic